==============================================================================
s13  The loop: what turns a model into an agent
      the model supplies pi; everything else is code you wrote
==============================================================================

3.1  The formalism, and where the model actually sits
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  eq:  a_t ~ pi(.|c_t);  s_{t+1} = T(s_t,a_t);  o_t = O(s_t,a_t);  c_{t+1} = c_t (+) (a_t,o_t)
       the model contributes pi and nothing else
  eq:  tau = min{ t : a_t = stop  or  t = N }
       the stopping time; autonomy is the question of who decides it

  task: 'index' names the record holding the answer, so no single call can
  succeed. The dependency is what makes the loop load-bearing rather than
  decorative.
  action space |A|                   6
  actions                            keys, get:index, get:record_3, get:record_7, get:record_9, stop
  shortest correct trajectory        get:index -> get:record_7 -> stop
  target value                       value_42

3.2  An LLM is the degenerate agent
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  configuration      trajectories  E[tau]  E[tool calls]  H(trajectory) bits  P(success)
  -----------------  ------------  ------  -------------  ------------------  ----------
  A = {stop}, N = 1             1       1              0                   0           0
  [PASS] with a single-element action space and N=1 the loop is exactly one model call
         one trajectory, zero tool calls, zero trajectory entropy
  # so 'LLM' and 'agent' are not two kinds of system. They are two settings of A and N. Everything the rest of this repo studies lives in the gap between this row and the next table, and none of it is inside the model.

3.3  Autonomy is a measurable quantity
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  eq:  H(trajectory) = - sum_z P(z) log2 P(z)  over complete trajectories z
       zero bits means the program decided everything; more bits means more of the decision was delegated to the policy
  configuration                     trajectories enumerated  H(traj) bits  E[tau]  P(success)
  --------------------------------  -----------------------  ------------  ------  ----------
  forced script (no choice at all)                        3             0       3           1
  3 actions permitted, skill 3.0                        127         1.609   3.043      0.9074
  6 actions permitted, skill 3.0                      19531         4.076   3.444      0.9069
  6 actions permitted, skill 1.0                      19531        10.125   4.149      0.4663
  6 actions, skill 1.0, T = 2.0                       19531        10.462   4.096      0.2961
  [PASS] constraining the action space strictly reduces trajectory entropy
         4.076 bits at |A|=6 against 1.609 bits at |A|=3, same skill
  [PASS] a near-deterministic policy has almost no autonomy by this measure
         H = 0.00e+00 bits for the forced script
  [PASS] raising temperature increases autonomy and reduces success together
         H 10.125 -> 10.462 bits while P(success) 0.4663 -> 0.2961
  # this is the trade every agent design makes, stated in bits. Autonomy is not a badge, it is the entropy you chose to permit, and you buy it with success rate. It is also the honest reply to 'is this really agentic': the question has a number, and the number is zero for a workflow with a fixed action sequence no matter what the marketing says.

3.4  Termination is a property of the policy, so the ceiling has to exist
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  eq:  tau ~ Geometric(q)  =>  E[tau] = 1/q,  P(tau > n) = (1-q)^n
       if the policy stops with probability q at each step independently
  stop_bias  q at step 1     1/q  E[tau] measured  P(hit ceiling)  P(success)
  ---------  -----------  ------  ---------------  --------------  ----------
         -3       0.0044  229.76            7.269          0.6982      0.9832
         -1       0.0313   31.96            5.174          0.1262      0.9047
          0       0.0807   12.39            4.041          0.0209      0.7742
          2       0.3935    2.54            2.179          0.0002        0.25
  [PASS] a policy reluctant to stop spends the entire step budget
         stop_bias -3.0: 70% of trajectories reach the ceiling, E[tau] = 7.27 of 8
  [PASS] eagerness to stop and competence trade off against each other
         stop_bias 2.0 terminates in 2.18 steps but succeeds 25.00% against 90.47% at stop_bias -1.0
  # E[tau] measured sits below 1/q because the loop truncates at N and because q is not constant across states -- the oracle bonus moves onto stop once the answer is in hand. The geometric is the intuition, not the model. The operational point stands either way: nothing in the policy guarantees termination, so the ceiling N is not a defensive nicety but the only thing standing between you and an unbounded bill.

3.5  Loop detection, and what it costs when the tool is not idempotent
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  policy                        loop detect  E[tau]  E[calls]  E[wasted calls]  P(success)
  ----------------------------  -----------  ------  --------  ---------------  ----------
  skill 3.0, reluctant to stop  off            4.57     3.788             0.88       0.986
  skill 3.0, reluctant to stop  on            3.634     3.151            0.563      0.9211
  skill 1.0, reluctant to stop  off           5.634     5.449            1.994       0.718
  skill 1.0, reluctant to stop  on            3.704     3.599            1.115       0.442
  [PASS] suppressing repeats of uninformative actions cuts steps and wasted calls
         E[tau] 4.570 -> 3.634, wasted calls 0.880 -> 0.563
  [PASS] it costs some success, because terminating early forfeits recovery
         P(success) 0.9860 -> 0.9211

  # the control is sound only because `get` is idempotent here: a repeat is provably uninformative, so ending the run loses no information that another step could have supplied. Change the tool and the reasoning inverts. Poll a queue, read a clock, retry a write that timed out, or re-read a record another process is updating, and the second identical call is exactly the call that carries new information. Repeat-suppression then silently converts a correct retry into a premature stop. The signature to deduplicate on is therefore not (tool, arguments) but (tool, arguments, world version), which is the same staleness problem S6 and S12 hit from the memory and cache sides -- three symptoms, one cause.

3.6  The loop's real cost: context grows, so prefill is paid again
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  eq:  |c_t| = |c_0| + sum_{i<t} (|a_i| + |o_i|)
       append-only, so prompt length is monotone in step count
  eq:  cost(N) = sum_{t=1..N} prefill(|c_t|)  ~  Theta(N^2) even when each call is linear
       N calls over a linearly growing prompt
  steps taken  prompt tokens  MACs this call  MACs cumulative  x one call
  -----------  -------------  --------------  ---------------  ----------
            1           1200       8.738e+12        8.738e+12           1
            2           1650       1.221e+13        2.095e+13         2.4
            4           2550       1.947e+13        5.620e+13         6.4
            8           4350       3.527e+13        1.730e+14        19.8
           16           7950       7.195e+13        6.158e+14        70.5
           32          15150       1.657e+14        2.528e+15       289.3
  [PASS] total loop cost grows faster than the number of steps
         32 steps cost 289.3x a single call, i.e. 9.04x more than 32 identical calls
  [PASS] and each individual call grows faster than the transcript does
         prompt grows 12.6x (1200 -> 15150 tokens) while the call costs 19.0x, because at 15k tokens the quadratic attention term is no longer negligible against 2d + d_ff = 24576
  # the reason this is survivable in practice is S11.5: the transcript is append-only, so every step's prompt is a prefix extension of the last and the KV cache carries almost all of it. That is also why history-compaction is a genuinely hard call rather than an obvious win -- it shortens the prompt, and it throws away the cache from the first edited token, which S12 prices properly.

3.7  Confirming the exact enumeration against sampling
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  quantity      exact  sampled (n=40,000)  difference   2 s.e.
  ----------  -------  ------------------  ----------  -------
  P(success)  0.88962             0.88935     0.00027  0.00313
  E[tau]       4.6968              4.7024      0.0056        -
  P(ceiling)  0.28571              0.2869     0.00119        -
  trajectories enumerated            19531
  probability mass unaccounted for   3.20e-13
  [PASS] the sampled estimate agrees with the exact value inside sampling error
         |difference| 0.00027 against 2 s.e. = 0.00313
  [PASS] the enumeration accounts for essentially all probability mass
         residual 3.20e-13
  # this is the section's own control. The exact numbers above are only worth quoting if the enumeration is right, and agreement with an independent sampler at 40,000 draws is the cheapest way to show it is. Note also what the comparison costs: the sampler needed 40,000 runs to pin P(success) to about half a percent. That is the regime every real agent evaluation lives in, and S12 is about surviving it.

